ally wrote a great deal  which is not read at all today in  academic philosophy departments or  taught on courses of course a big chunk  of that of his history is in his day he  actually became famous as a historian  before he did as a philosopher his six  volume history of england was  widely praised and you know it was was  meant to be the the greatest history  written to date of england but also he  wrote a large number of essays on  practical issues political issues  topical issues  and these essays have kind of slipped  into the background they're they're put  they are available  in fact ever pretty much everything he  wrote apart from his letters and  correspondence is available online at a  wonderful website hume texts online but  they're not really studied  but the other thing is that the way in  which we approach philosophy  these days is both sort of a historical  we might say a biographical we don't  really take much interest in the lives  of the philosophers themselves  we look at their ideas and we take their  ideas to  be  freestanding things that we can examine  on their own merits  and yeah the the life and times are  almost irrelevant now i think with hume  if you look at his work and his life  together i think you get a different  kind of picture and of course we know  quite a lot about his life they've been  mosner wrote the  definitive early definitive kind of  biography of him  there's been some other ones since  and there's quite a lot of  correspondence that survives from hume's  time as well so in his letters we find  out quite a lot about what he was  thinking and what he was saying  so what i want to kind of convince you  of really is that actually hume is a  practical philosopher as well as an  interesting theoretical one and there  are very interesting lessons we can  learn from him  and i've just selected 12  life lessons this is a nice round number  here's the first one  be stoical  with a small s  but don't be a stoic  and i rather like this one  because i don't deliberately want to be  provocative but the recently has been  real revival in stoicism a lot of books  published about it  kind of uh  sometimes referred to as the new  stoicism a lot of people saying that you  know in the stoics we have a kind of a  blueprint for how to live  and many books have been written with  that kind of subtext or not even subtext  as an explicit  claim  and  a lot of it sounds wonderful yeah it  always sounds very persuasive lots of  very memorable pithy quotes you get from  the stoics uh telling us things like how  we should not become too attached to  things that are beyond our control not  worry about things that we can't change  focus on what we  can change and so forth and a lot of  that sounds like a very very uh good  sense but actually i think at the end of  the day  uh i don't i'm not persuaded any of us  should be stoics and i don't think  hume was a stoic either  although hume would agree with a lot of  those kind of things you hear repeated  as being good stoic wisdom  but he wasn't a stoke himself so what do  we know about this well this is  interesting again because as a young man  he was was very earnest and and very  keen  to to get on with things and study and  become a man of letters as he put it  he was born in the early 18th century  born actually in edinburgh but he grew  up in a place called churnside near the  scottish borders  nine worlds was the family estate his  family were not rich by the standards of  the middle classes of the time but of  course by the standards of the ninety  percent of the population of course they  were they had their own houses in two  different places  uh cooks and things like this and it was  a very isolated place chancellor he grew  up there he went to university i think  it was been about 12 when he went to  university this was very very normal at  the time a very strange kind of thing  the universities were at the time he  didn't graduate but again that was  completely normal as well because in  order to graduate uh but  when you graduated the tutor basically  got some kind of fee and only certain  tutors were quite  able to get that so a lot of the time  people didn't bother and he wasn't  really that impressed by his university  days actually and concluded that um  there's nothing that you can learn from  a professor which you couldn't pick up  from a book  and that was perhaps something which  still remains true today so maybe you  should stop listening now and just read  a book instead but anyway he did that he  came back and then he set himself to  work very seriously he was very earnest  he was very very interested in the  stoics  and he read them very seriously but he  ultimately concluded that  the stoics weren't for him they weren't  right and why was that well  here's a key quote i think  hume puts things so well if you read him  initially it can sometimes be a bit  off-putting he in his 18th century prose  has a lot of kind of long sentences  slightly strange spellings etc etc if  you sort of get into the rhythm of it  he's actually a wonderful writer great  stylist and very very clear  and here's his kind of the bottom line  about why you shouldn't be a stoic with  a capital s philosophers have  endeavoured to render happiness entirely  independent of everything external that  degree of perfection is impossible to be  attained now that really is the hard  message of stoicism which i think a lot  of modern advocates kind of gloss over  if there's anybody in the room who's a  modern stoic as it were you can feel  free to argue with me later i know that  people who say this simply not true the  stoics did think that things like our  relationships could be of value even  money pleasure all the things that we  normally take pleasure in the stoics did  think they could be of value but they  weren't of the highest value and we had  to be careful about how we related to  them it's how we related to them that  mattered but actually i think if you  look at what the stoics actually said  i think that's making it sound a bit  more palatable than it really is on  their theory of value the only thing  that really mattered was our intellect  it was logos and reason and that was the  only thing within our control and  everything else was was relegated and  they called them preferred indifference  in the sense that it was okay to take a  certain amount of pleasure of them if  they came along or a part of your life  but you mustn't get attached to them and  you mustn't see them have been great  importance and that's why you've got  these fairly sort of hard  passages which are there in black and  white such as  i always forget which which stoic said  what but you know that when you kiss  your wife and you kiss your children you  should remind yourself that they're just  flesh and blood and they will die so  that you are not too upset when they do  die so it's this kind of thing of making  yourself invulnerable to things that are  not in your control as being the key to  happiness and and as human said here  therefore rendering happiness entirely  independent of everything external  now you can see why you would want to  make your happiness independent of  everything external wouldn't that be  wonderful that would mean your happiness  you'd be invulnerable essentially  nothing that happened to you  could affect your happiness  uh your happiness would only be affected  by what was internal to you your own  ways of thinking and so forth so it's a  very very attractive option  but human says here the degree of  perfection is impossible to be attained  because you know that's just not a human  life frankly and if you look at how the  stoics actually lived you find that they  really didn't live up to what they're  advocating here anyway most of the time  they did value things external to them  um a few of them had quite a bit of  money for a start  now  here's another question which i think  takes this a little bit further makes it  a little bit clearer  the real idea of becoming a stoic with a  capital s  is  essentially to stop extinguish or to  dampen down those emotions and passions  which make us attached to things that  are beyond our control right  now here's what hume says those refined  reflections which philosophy suggests to  us  is that commonly they cannot diminish or  extinguish our vicious passions without  diminishing or extinguishing such as our  virtuous and rendering the mind totally  indifferent and unactive  so what he was really suggesting here  is that it's kind of not possible just  like selectively switch off the emotions  which lead us to unhappiness and  distress etc without the same time  switching off all those other emotions  which give us the joys in life so if you  go back to relationships you know you  can't turn off  the emotions which are going to make you  distraught and heartbroken if those  close to you die  without also switching off precisely  those emotions which make it such a  delight to be with them  when they're alive so in that first  quote  he says that that degree of perfection  is impossible to be attained i think  really the conclusion he's also got  is that it it's not desirable to attain  it you wouldn't want to become the kind  of person that the stoics claim we  should be  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai.tv for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas  you

